Two-sum, executed:

```go
func twoSum(nums []int, target int) [2]int {
    seen := make(map[int]int)
    for i, n := range nums {
        if j, ok := seen[target-n]; ok {
            return [2]int{j, i}
        }
        seen[n] = i
    }
    return [2]int{-1, -1}
}
// twoSum([]int{2,7,11,15}, 9) → [0, 1] — verified
```

The pattern: for each element, check 'have I seen the complement (target - n)?' in a hash map — O(1) per check. One pass, O(n) time, O(n) space. The alternative (check every pair) is O(n²) — the map trade: space for time.

The counter pattern:
```go
freq := make(map[string]int)
for _, word := range words { freq[word]++ }
// anagram check: count chars, compare maps
```

The anagram check via counter, executed:
```go
func isAnagram(a, b string) bool {
    if len(a) != len(b) { return false }
    counts := make(map[rune]int)
    for _, r := range a { counts[r]++ }
    for _, r := range b { counts[r]-- }
    for _, v := range counts { if v != 0 { return false } }
    return true
}
// isAnagram("listen", "silent") → true — verified
```

The patterns this enables: deduplication (the presence check), grouping (map[string][]T), indexing (map[record.ID] = position), memoization (map[args]result). The hash map is THE tool that converts 'nested loop over pairs' into 'single loop + lookup.'
